<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ram Khamhaeng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1237–1298</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Ram Khamhaeng the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ram Khamhaeng was the third king of Sukhothai, reigning from about 1279 to 1298, under whom the Thai kingdom expanded widely. Tradition credits him with creating the Thai script, recorded in the Ram Khamhaeng inscription. He was an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangrai">Mangrai</a> of Lan Na and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngam-mueang">Ngam Mueang</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a> and is said to have helped found Chiang Mai in 1296.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng.jpg" alt="Ram Khamhaeng" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. Feel free to use any of my images, but please mention me as the author ... · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ram_Khamhaeng_the_Great_(I).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
